    "body": "Two popemobiles that will be used during Pope Leo XIV’s visit to Peru arrived on Monday from Brazil aboard a Latam Airlines Group freighter aircraft, part of a special logistics operation carried out under contract to Peru’s Ministry of Foreign Affairs and in coordination with General Motors.\nThe two Chevrolet vehicles, weighing three tons each and specially modified with protective features such as bulletproof glass, were transported by Latam Cargo from Varacopos International Airport in São Paulo to Lima, Peru’s capital, onboard a Boeing 767-300 freighter, the airline said in a news release.\nThe popemobiles were manufactured at the General Motors plant in São Bernardo do Campo near São Paulo.\nPope Leo is scheduled to visit Peru from Nov. 11 to Nov. 17 as part of a 12-day journey to Latin America that will include stops in Uruguay and Argentina. The Pope is scheduled to visit four cities in Peru. During the trip, the Pope will celebrate Mass, meet with local church communities and address civil authorities, according to the Vatican.\nLatam is the largest airline in South America and its freighter division operates 19 Boeing 767-300 converted cargo jets.\nDue to the vehicles’ dimensions and specific characteristics, the popemobile operation required between six and eight hours of preparation on the airport ramp, as well as specialized procedures to ensure their proper handling and transportation, Latam said.\nAs is normal for moving vehicles, Latam had to calculate weight distribution inside the aircraft, secure the vehicles with specialized tie-down systems on cargo platforms, and use hydraulic equipment for loading. About 20 people were required on either end of the trip for loading and unloading. The shipment also required extra security protocols to be followed.\nClick here for more FreightWaves/American Shipper stories by Eric Kulisch.\nWrite to Eric Kulisch at ekulisch@freightwaves.com .\nRELATED STORIES:\nBoeing secures lessor order for 767 passenger-to-freighter conversion\nAirbus sees 46% freighter increase by 2045 amid stable air cargo growth",
